       <h1>Chelsea Manager Xabi Alonso Struggles for Identity After Sunderland Defeat</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#sports</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Chelsea manager Xabi Alonso faces growing scrutiny after his team suffered a 1-0 defeat to Sunderland, leaving the club with five wins in 10 Premier League matches. Alonso's frequent changes to personnel and formation have drawn comparisons to the 'Tinkerman' moniker, as the side continues to struggle for a consistent tactical identity.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/chelsea-manager-xabi-alonso-struggles-for-identity-after-sun.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>During the match, Enzo le Fee scored the decisive goal via a penalty awarded after a foul by Levi Colwill. Although Alonso expressed frustration with his team's defensive errors, he avoided criticizing officials, instead urging his players to execute the game plan more effectively.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Covers the tactical struggles and manager's post-match comments accurately within the word count.</p>
