     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Guardian Environment · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Cop31 Summit Opens in Turkey Amidst El Niño Crisis, Demands Tangible Climate Progress</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Climate Change</span> <span>#Cop31</span> <span>#El Niño</span> <span>#Climate Finance</span></div>
       <p>The Cop31 summit has commenced in Turkey, gathering scores of world leaders and representatives from close to 190 countries for critical UN climate talks. The host nation has emphasized that the high-stakes meeting must yield &quot;a tangible outcome... that people can see is real.&quot; These discussions unfold as countries worldwide grapple with the strongest El Niño weather system on record, which, supercharged by fossil fuels, is projected to cause hundreds of thousands of deaths and trillions of dollars in damage. Temperatures have already broken records this year, surpassing the 1.5 degrees Celsius limit above preindustrial levels set by the 2015 Paris climate agreement, highlighting the urgency of the talks.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/cop31-summit-opens-in-turkey-amidst-el-nino-crisis-demands-t.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Significant friction is anticipated between the rich and poor nations over accountability for the climate crisis impacts and the slow progress in cutting greenhouse gas emissions. Adão Soares Barbosa, chair of the Least Developed Countries, a group of 44 nations, stated that &quot;keeping 1.5C within reach... delivering accessible and predictable climate finance, strengthening adaptation, and addressing loss and damage... are not political choices but matters of survival.&quot; He underscored that the Paris Agreement's credibility would be measured by &quot;commitments fulfilled, and lives protected in vulnerable countries.&quot; Unusually, the formal negotiations will be presided over by Australia, despite the summit being held in the Turkish city of Antalya, a compromise reached last year. Australia's climate and energy minister, Chris Bowen, who is president of negotiations for Cop31, told The Guardian that a &quot;strong, ambitious outcome&quot; is sought to address the existential threat faced by low-lying Pacific countries, noting that &quot;only 0.2% of the world’s available climate finance goes to small island developing states for adaptation.&quot;</p><p>The summit sees a notable absentee, with the United States refusing to send a delegation for the second consecutive year, as a White House spokesperson cited a disinterest in &quot;extremist climate policies.&quot; This stance contrasts with the presence of 20 Democratic members of Congress, buoyed by recent US midterm successes, aiming to counter attempts to dismantle pollution rules and withhold clean energy financial support. Global climate impacts are becoming increasingly evident; last summer, over 50,000 people died in Europe due to heatwaves, severe wildfire smoke engulfed parts of North America, and a glacier collapse in Nepal triggered a landslide causing more than 1,500 deaths. The El Niño event, described as &quot;staggering&quot; by scientists, continues to strengthen and has yet to reach its peak.</p><p>Despite the slow pace of global emissions reductions, recent geopolitical events, such as the Iran war, have prompted many countries to perceive fossil fuels as expensive and vulnerable to supply shocks, while renewable energy prices continue to decline. Simon Stiell, the UN’s climate chief, observed that &quot;almost every major economy... now see climate action as a central driver of economic growth, jobs, and affordable and secure energy.” Host nation Turkey advocates for a broader switch to electric technologies like electric vehicles and heat pumps, citing their superior efficiency over fossil fuel counterparts. A proposal to achieve 35% of global final energy from electricity by 2035 is on the table, though it faces opposition, underscoring the ongoing challenges and opportunities at Cop31 to forge tangible progress against the intensifying climate crisis.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft successfully synthesizes the provided full text, capturing the political context, climate impacts, and summit objectives.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/cop31-climate-summit-deliver-progress-real-host-says" rel="noopener">Guardian Environment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
