       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENTERTAINMENT</span> · TOI Entertainment · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Dipika Chikhlia Comments on Sai Pallavi's Action Scenes in 'Ramayana'</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#entertainment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Veteran actress Dipika Chikhlia, known for her role in the 1987 'Ramayan' series, recently addressed public discourse regarding combat sequences performed by Sai Pallavi in Nitesh Tiwari’s 'Ramayana: Part One'. Chikhlia noted that while such action scenes were not part of the Valmiki or Tulsidas versions she is familiar with, she acknowledged the existence of over 300 variations of the epic.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/dipika-chikhlia-comments-on-sai-pallavis-action-scenes-in-ra.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Director Nitesh Tiwari previously clarified that the film’s interpretation of Sita is inspired by the 'Adbhuta Ramayana', which depicts her with divine feminine energy. Chikhlia stated she plans to watch the film following the Diwali festival.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes Dipika Chikhlia's stance and the director's explanation for the creative choices in the film.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/entertainment/hindi/bollywood/news/the-og-sita-dipika-chikhlia-reacts-to-sai-pallavis-combat-sequences-in-ramayana-i-dont-remember-such-scenes-in-valmiki-or-tulsidas-ramayana/articleshow/135662447.cms" rel="noopener">TOI Entertainment</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
