     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Gita Gopinath Warns $1 Trillion AI Spending Risks Market Correction, US Recession</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#AI Investment</span> <span>#Market Correction</span> <span>#US Economy</span></div>
       <p>Gita Gopinath, a Harvard professor and former chief economist of the International Monetary Fund (IMF), has issued a warning regarding the current artificial intelligence (AI) investment boom. Speaking on Raj Shamani's podcast, Gopinath cautioned that the market could face a significant correction if companies fail to generate sufficient profits to recoup the nearly $1 trillion in expected global AI spending in 2026 alone. This substantial investment is being poured into infrastructure and development, with some companies even cutting jobs to reallocate resources towards AI initiatives, while investors actively chase exposure in markets like the United States, South Korea, and Taiwan.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/gita-gopinath-warns-1-trillion-ai-spending-risks-market-corr.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Gopinath expressed her personal belief that &quot;there will be a correction,&quot; although she noted it is &quot;impossible to tell when that correction will happen.&quot; Her concern stems from the challenge for AI labs and companies to achieve the kind of profits necessary to recover such immense investments amidst intense industry competition. She questioned, &quot;It's hard to explain how each of these labs and companies who are investing close to a trillion dollars now globally in AI just in 2026 will be able to make the kind of profits that are needed to recover that investment.&quot;</p><p>Globally, growth projections for 2025 and 2026 remain stable, masking underlying shifts. The AI boom is currently driving investment and growth, yet geopolitical tensions, tariffs, and the US-Iran conflict in West Asia simultaneously weigh on economies. This dynamic creates a visible divide: Taiwan and South Korea, linked to the AI supply chain, are growing at roughly twice their usual pace, with Taiwan's statistics agency raising its 2026 growth forecast to 11.05%, the fastest annual growth since 1987. Conversely, West Asia economies, such as Saudi Arabia and the UAE, face growth downgrades. The United States, however, is growing at or slightly above expectations, with AI investment offsetting some tariff-related drag.</p><p>Gopinath described the current global economic arrangement as a &quot;gamble.&quot; Despite trade accounting for its highest-ever share of global GDP, making the world more interconnected, geopolitical power is more dispersed than during the Cold War, with the US, China, and India shaping the landscape but lacking coordination on AI safety. This interconnectedness also manifests in a circular flow of money within the AI ecosystem; for instance, NVIDIA invests in AI labs, which then purchase NVIDIA chips. Gopinath warned that the collapse of NVIDIA or an AI lab could have significant implications for the wider market, highlighting these unseen linkages.</p><p>For ordinary taxpayers and savers, a sharp stock-market correction, potentially triggered by the AI investment bubble, could have direct consequences. Gopinath warned that such an event could push US growth to zero or slightly negative, potentially triggering a recession. While she suggested such a recession, if purely driven by market losses, might be short-lived, it underscores the fragility. Currently, the AI boom is supporting the US economy through wealth gains and increased spending, making any correction a critical event to monitor.</p><p>What to watch: The profitability of AI investments and broader market stability.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft comprehensively captures all key data points, specific growth forecasts, and the economic concerns raised by Gopinath without unnecessary padding.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/hard-to-recover-the-investment-gita-gopinath-warns-1-trillion-ai-bet-could-correct-flags-us-recession-risk-11794138794202.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
