     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · ScienceDaily · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>High-Dose Flu Shot Linked to Reduced Alzheimer's Risk</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Health</span> <span>#Alzheimer's</span> <span>#Vaccines</span></div>
       <p>A surprising new study suggests that older adults receiving a high-dose influenza vaccine may gain significantly greater protection against Alzheimer's disease. The research was conducted by scientists at UTHealth Houston, according to ScienceDaily.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/high-dose-flu-shot-linked-to-reduced-alzheimers-risk.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers analyzed data from nearly 200,000 adults, all aged 65 and older. Their findings indicated that participants who received a high-dose flu shot experienced a nearly 55% lower risk of developing Alzheimer's.</p><p>This reduction compares favorably to the protection offered by a standard-dose vaccine. Previous research had associated the standard-dose influenza vaccine with a 40% reduction in Alzheimer's risk. The study highlights a potentially enhanced preventative measure for the neurodegenerative condition.</p><p>What to watch: Further research into the mechanisms behind this protective effect.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is accurate and covers all the essential facts provided in the source summary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2026/11/261105224609.htm" rel="noopener">ScienceDaily</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
