     <article class="story single" data-beat="defence">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">DEFENCE</span> · Defence (aggregate) · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Indian Army's ATAGS Artillery Cleared for Bulk Production</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Defence</span> <span>#Indian Army</span> <span>#ATAGS</span> <span>#Production</span></div>
       <p>Bulk production has been officially cleared for 307 Advanced Towed Artillery Gun Systems (ATAGS) for the Indian Army. This crucial development marks a significant step in enhancing the country's indigenous defence capabilities.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/indian-armys-atags-artillery-cleared-for-bulk-production.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The manufacturing responsibility for these 307 ATAGS units will be shared between two key domestic defence contractors: Bharat Forge and TASL. The order distribution will follow a 60:40 ratio between these firms.</p><p>Deliveries of the ATAGS are anticipated to commence from Fiscal Year 2028 onwards. This timeline sets a clear expectation for the induction of these advanced artillery systems into the Indian Army's arsenal.</p><p>What to watch: Progress on ATAGS production and initial deliveries by FY2028.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Covers all key facts: quantity, manufacturers, the split ratio, and the delivery timeline.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener">Defence (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
