       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Karnataka CM Alleges Illegal Ethanol Mixing in Petrol</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Karnataka Chief Minister DK Shivakumar has accused oil marketing companies of excessive ethanol blending in petrol, claiming it causes engine failures and reduced vehicle mileage. The state government reported findings of ethanol levels reaching up to 31.2% in certain samples, exceeding the prescribed 20% limit. Shivakumar alleged that illegal mixing is occurring at petrol pumps to boost profits.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/karnataka-cm-alleges-illegal-ethanol-mixing-in-petrol.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Public sector oil companies including Indian Oil and Bharat Petroleum rejected these allegations in a joint statement, asserting that their fuel meets Bureau of Indian Standards specifications. The companies further challenged the state's testing methodology, citing improper sampling procedures during the government's October 2026 exercise.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief effectively summarizes the conflict, the specific allegations, and the industry response.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/engines-failing-dks-accuses-centre-of-cover-up-amid-ethanol-belnding-row/articleshow/135652976.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
