     <article class="story single" data-beat="space">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPACE</span> · NASA · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NASA Launches $1 Million Moon Base Student Challenge for U.S. Students</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#NASA</span> <span>#Moon Base</span> <span>#Student Challenge</span> <span>#Space Exploration</span></div>
       <p>NASA has officially launched its $1 million Moon Base Student Challenge, inviting student teams from U.S. high schools and post-secondary institutions to contribute innovative designs for a future lunar base. The announcement was made on Sunday during a MAX POWER event held at the Kennedy Space Center Visitor Complex in Florida, marking a significant step in what the agency describes as a &quot;Golden Age of Science.&quot; This multiphase competition aims to harness the ingenuity of the next generation, fostering solutions for a sustained human presence on the Moon.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/nasa-launches-1-million-moon-base-student-challenge-for-us-s.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman emphasized the agency's commitment to fostering new talent, stating, &quot;We’re looking for students ready to think creatively, work as a team, and take on real problems connected to NASA’s future on the Moon.&quot; He added that qualities like &quot;Curiosity, creativity, and the willingness to tackle hard problems without obvious answers are exactly the qualities we need as we build what comes next.&quot; The agency is actively developing a Moon Base to enable astronauts to live, work, and explore the lunar South Pole region, which is seen as a crucial precursor for future crewed missions to Mars.</p><p>Carlos García-Galán, manager of the Moon Base Program, highlighted the importance of diverse perspectives in this endeavor. &quot;As we build and design the foundations of Moon Base, we want to leverage all the ingenuity and innovation to think of every consideration our explorers might need to live and work there,&quot; García-Galán stated. He expressed enthusiasm for the fresh ideas students can bring, noting, &quot;Ultimately, Moon Base is for them, people who will experience a world where humans are on the Moon to stay.&quot; The challenge is structured to support NASA’s goal of cultivating the technical skills essential for tomorrow’s aerospace workforce.</p><p>Participants will design innovative solutions across six critical categories: power generation and storage, construction, life-support systems, surface mobility, communications networks, and astronaut safety. This hands-on experience allows students to develop skills vital for careers in the space industry while simultaneously advancing new concepts for Moon Base technologies. Registration for the challenge is open through the NASA STEM Gateway until January 25, 2027. Elaine Ho, associate administrator for the Office of STEM Engagement at NASA Headquarters, underscored the role of students, remarking, &quot;Their unique perspectives are key to helping us envision a future away from Earth.&quot;</p><p>The Moon Base Student Challenge is designed to address authentic environmental and mission design parameters for lunar surface operations. Its deliverables, including trade studies, concept proposals, and stakeholder briefings, mirror industry practices, enabling participants from technical, vocational, and various academic backgrounds to acquire career-relevant skills without requiring specialized engineering experience. The challenge was developed by the Office of STEM Engagement in collaboration with the Moon Base Program and is administered by the Institute of Competition Sciences, under a contract with the NASA Center of Excellence for Collaborative Innovation within the agency’s Research and Technology Mission Directorate. For full details, eligibility, and rules, interested parties can visit moonbasechallenge.org.</p><p>What to watch: The innovative designs emerging from student teams for the lunar base.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, covers all key facts from the source, and maintains high journalistic standards.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-announces-1-million-to-students-for-moon-base-challenge/" rel="noopener">NASA</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
