     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-11-08 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Pakistan Pacer Ali Raza Strikes Early as India Stumble in U19 Asia Cup 2026 Clash</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Sports</span> <span>#Cricket</span> <span>#U19 Asia Cup</span> <span>#India vs Pakistan</span></div>
       <p>In the highly anticipated Men's U19 Asia Cup 2026 match on Sunday, India's captain Lakshya Raichandani won the toss and elected to bat first against arch-rivals Pakistan. Both teams deployed the same Playing XIs that had secured victories in their campaign openers, with India having outplayed Nepal by 10 wickets and Pakistan triumphing over the UAE by nine wickets. However, India's innings got off to a turbulent start, as Pakistan's pacer Ali Raza made an immediate impact. In just the second ball of the innings, Raza stunned captain Raichandani, dismissing him for a duck with a delivery that nipped back sharply to disturb the off stump, leaving India at 0/1.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/pakistan-pacer-ali-raza-strikes-early-as-india-stumble-in-u1.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The pressure intensified as Ali Raza continued his fiery spell, securing Pakistan's second wicket by dismissing VK Vineeth for 8 runs at 8.4 overs, bringing India to 21/2. Raza utilized the bounce effectively, troubling Vineeth, who ultimately edged the ball to wicketkeeper Hamza Zahoor for an easy catch. Following Vineeth's departure, Aaryavir Sehwag was joined by Yashbardhan Chauhan at the crease. The duo attempted to stabilize the innings, with Ali Raza delivering a good over, conceding just a single run. However, the partnership was short-lived. Rizwanullah then joined the attack in style, claiming Pakistan's third wicket by cleaning up Yashbardhan Chauhan for just 1 run in the 11.4th over. Rizwanullah found the gap between Chauhan's legs and knocked down the stumps; a third umpire review confirmed the pacer's leg was on the line, solidifying the dismissal and pushing India to 22/3.</p><p>With India struggling at three wickets down, Aaryavir Sehwag, who had impressed with an unbeaten knock of 70 against Nepal, was joined by Arjun Rajput, tasked with forging a stable partnership. Pakistan's bowlers, including Mohammad Sayyam and Ali Raza, maintained a tight grip on the scoring rate. Sayyam bowled a terrific over, conceding only two runs early on, and later delivered a maiden over, further increasing pressure on the Indian batters. Despite the challenges, Sehwag managed to break the shackles with a fiery boundary off Ali Raza, showcasing a glimpse of his namesake Virender Sehwag's aggressive style.</p><p>The partnership started to gain momentum, with the duo scoring four runs off Mohammad Sayyam's over, moving India to 7/1 by the second over. By the fourth over, India was 9/1, struggling against the pace of Pakistan's bowlers. However, a significant turning point came in the 13th over, where Sehwag and Rajput combined to score 12 runs off Hasnain Abbas Dar, including a boundary from Arjun and a huge maximum from Sehwag, providing a much-needed sigh of relief for India and taking the score to 34/3. As the innings progressed, they continued to build, scoring two runs off Niqab Shafiq's over to reach 38/3 after 15 overs, indicating a growing stability in their partnership amidst Pakistan's aggressive bowling.</p><p>What to watch: The resilience of Sehwag and Rajput and whether India can post a competitive total.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent play-by-play reporting that synthesizes the provided live updates into a coherent match narrative.</p>
