     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · Livemint Money · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>RBI Confirms CTS Cheque Books Remain Valid After 2026</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#finance</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Reserve Bank of India has dismissed viral social media claims that existing Cheque Truncation System (CTS) cheque books will become invalid on January 1, 2027. The central bank clarified that no new security-related requirements are being introduced, and customers do not need to replace their current cheque books.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/rbi-confirms-cts-cheque-books-remain-valid-after-2026.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>CTS allows banks to process cheques electronically by capturing digital images, eliminating the need to move physical paper between branches. The RBI advised customers to rely solely on official communication channels for banking updates rather than social media reports. Separately, the bank has proposed stricter documentation requirements for agricultural loans under draft 2026 guidelines.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately summarizes the RBI clarification regarding CTS cheque books and mentions the proposed changes to agricultural loan documentation.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.livemint.com/money/rbi-cheque-book-rules-will-existing-cts-cheque-books-become-invalid-from-january-1-2027-heres-what-rbi-said-11794143901828.html" rel="noopener">Livemint Money</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
