     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-11-08 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>UK Growth Minister Confirms Continued AI Data Center Expansion</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>UK growth minister Dan Tomlinson announced that data center construction will proceed as &quot;essential infrastructure&quot; to support the country's growing AI economy. The government aims to reach a computing capacity of 6GW by 2030, building on the &quot;AI Growth Zones&quot; initiative established in 2025.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-08/uk-growth-minister-confirms-continued-ai-data-center-expansi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Despite public protests at construction sites, the UK plans a more nuanced approach than the US, requiring developers to invest in local communities and create jobs. Officials intend to provide stricter oversight for these projects compared to the rapid, less regulated development seen in parts of the United States.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is concise, accurate, and includes all the key figures and policy context provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2310304/uk-has-no-plans-to-slow-down-ai-data-center-construction/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
